const chapterTitles = ["When Neurodivergence Is Part of the Picture","Before We Decide What It Means","What Was Possible in That Moment?","The Nervous System and Its Protectors","When a Demand Becomes a Threat","When Both People Need Something Different","When Similar Behaviour Has Different Roots","Before It Becomes a Crisis","Accountability Without Blame","What Children Are Asked to Carry","A Life That Makes Room"];
const chapterParts = ["Part I — Understanding the Whole Picture","Part I — Understanding the Whole Picture","Part I — Understanding the Whole Picture","Part I — Understanding the Whole Picture","Part II — Understanding the Collision","Part II — Understanding the Collision","Part II — Understanding the Collision","Part III — Changing Everyday Life","Part III — Changing Everyday Life","Part III — Changing Everyday Life","Part III — Changing Everyday Life"];
const chapterTexts = ["You can receive treatment that helps and still have important needs that nobody has identified. You can learn to recognise an emotional pattern while continuing to struggle with sound, transitions, conversation or the work of organising a day. You can become more skilled at getting through difficult moments and still return, every morning, to circumstances that exceed what you can sustain.\n\nUnderstanding the whole picture begins with making room for these experiences together. What brings you into care matters. So does what it takes to live between appointments.\n\n### The emergency beacon\n\nWhen distress becomes urgent, people look towards it. A clinician may need to respond to intense emotional pain, repeated crises, impulsive behaviour or immediate risk. A diagnosis can help organise that response. It can give a person language, access to treatment and an explanation for experiences that previously felt bewildering.\n\nBut attention can remain fixed on the emergency beacon. Future appointments may ask how well you are managing the same symptoms. Your account of a difficult week may be heard through a diagnosis already in the record. The question of what your everyday environment asks of you may receive less attention.\n\nConsider a woman diagnosed with borderline personality disorder, or BPD. Treatment may help her understand fear of abandonment, emotional instability or patterns in relationships. She may recognise herself in that account. Meanwhile, longstanding sensory sensitivities, difficulty with transitions or the effort of navigating social expectations may remain unexplored. Autism or ADHD might also be part of her experience.\n\nResearch with adults diagnosed with BPD before autism describes varied ways of understanding that diagnostic sequence. Some participants reconsidered their earlier diagnosis. Others described a more complicated relationship between the two. Their accounts support asking what an existing diagnosis explains and what still needs attention. [Study of adults diagnosed with BPD before autism](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13159771/)\n\nIn this companion, the emergency beacon is a way to think about attention. It asks whether the most visible distress has become the centre of the entire explanation. A useful question for care is, “What would we ask about if we were also trying to understand an ordinary Tuesday?”\n\n### A developmental history belongs in the conversation\n\nNeurodivergence is an umbrella term used for differences in how people think, process and experience the world. This companion focuses particularly on autism, ADHD and their co-occurrence, often called AuDHD. A developmental history considers patterns across a person's life, including experiences that were present long before their most recent crisis.\n\nThink about the situations you have always found unusually demanding. Perhaps you needed a long period alone after school. Perhaps starting homework required far more support than understanding it. Perhaps you learnt conversation by preparing responses, or had difficulty keeping track of instructions while somebody continued speaking. These recollections give an assessor questions to explore alongside the rest of your history.\n\nThe account may have gaps. You may have few records, limited contact with family or difficulty remembering how you coped. You may have been described as capable because the work eventually got done. An assessment can consider what information is available and where uncertainty remains. You can begin by describing the needs you encounter now.\n\n### More than one thing can be happening\n\nAnxiety, depression, obsessive-compulsive disorder, trauma-related disorders and personality disorders each raise their own questions. Experiences may overlap in appearance while differing in purpose, timing or what helps. Several conditions may also coexist.\n\nA repeated action, for example, might provide enjoyment or predictability. It might also be connected with an intrusive fear and an attempt to prevent something terrible. Understanding it requires asking what the person experiences before, during and after it. The action's appearance gives us a starting point.\n\nA fuller account can lead to several outcomes. An earlier diagnosis may remain useful alongside a newly recognised condition. It may be reconsidered. A person may need further assessment before anyone can confidently explain how their difficulties fit together. Research on perceived psychiatric misdiagnosis among autistic adults highlights how frequently participants questioned diagnoses received before autism was recognised, particularly among women. Those reports describe participants' perceptions, which are valuable information and a distinct form of evidence from an independent diagnostic reassessment. [Study of perceived psychiatric misdiagnosis](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11001629/)\n\nThe practical question is what each part of the account changes. Does it alter treatment? Does it explain an access need? Does it help you recognise an earlier sign of difficulty? Does it identify something that requires separate care?\n\n### The experience of living with unmet needs\n\nThere can be painful consequences to having needs that other people repeatedly misunderstand. A child who covers their ears may be made to remain in the room. A request for an explanation may be treated as insolence. An adult who has worked hard to conceal distress may be told that a sudden inability to continue makes no sense.\n\nOver time, a person may begin preparing for disbelief as well as for the situation itself. Asking for help can carry memories of what happened the last time. A new classroom, workplace or relationship can contain reminders of earlier experiences, even when the people there have different intentions.\n\nAutistic adults and caregivers in qualitative research have described a broad range of potentially traumatic experiences, including bullying, exclusion and experiences shaped by sensory and communication differences. This invites careful attention to what happened from the person's perspective. It also leaves clinical questions about symptoms, duration, impairment and diagnosis to be explored in their own right. [Study of potential sources of childhood trauma](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9597164/)\n\nSomeone can say, “That experience frightened me and changed how I respond,” before having an agreed diagnostic explanation for it. Their account gives care something concrete to investigate. What did they learn to anticipate? What situations now feel difficult? What happens when they try to leave or ask for relief?\n\n### The stack stacks fast\n\nNow add a relationship. Both adults may be neurodivergent. Both may carry trauma histories. Each may be trying to protect a limited amount of energy while sharing a home, earning money, managing health and maintaining a connection.\n\nAdd children with additional support needs. School communication, appointments, transitions, food, transport, sleep and emotional support all require somebody's attention. Work continues while a child is having a difficult day. An adult's own appointment may be postponed to attend another one. Recovery gets moved into whatever time remains.\n\nThese demands interact. A late appointment can disrupt dinner, which changes a child's routine, which adds more talking and decisions at the point when an adult needs quiet. Another adult may also need predictability and feel increasingly anxious while the plan remains unsettled. What eventually looks like one argument may have arrived after hours of adaptation.\n\nChildren have their own experiences inside this arrangement. One may ask repeatedly because they need a clear answer. Another may stop asking because they have noticed how much the adults are carrying. Understanding family load includes asking what each person needs and which responsibilities belong with adults, services and the wider support network.\n\nThere will be situations where the work genuinely exceeds what the available people can sustain. A relationship conversation can identify that problem. Solving it may also require practical help, changes at work, school support, financial assistance or accessible healthcare. The household deserves an account that includes its resources as well as its responses.\n\n### Care that can hold the whole picture\n\nYou might want treatment for distress and adjustments that make treatment accessible. Those needs can be discussed together. Guidance for autistic adults recognises coexisting mental health conditions and recommends adapting the delivery of psychological interventions, including attention to communication and the environment. [NICE guidance on autism in adults](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg142/chapter/recommendations)\n\nA useful appointment may ask what helped, what remained difficult and what happened before the crisis. It may consider a quieter space, a written summary, breaks or a different way to describe symptoms. It may also reveal a separate difficulty that needs assessment. The aim is an account detailed enough to guide care in the life you actually lead.\n\n### A whole-picture inventory\n\nYou can make a few notes in any format that is easy to use. Choose the questions that matter now.\n\n- Which diagnoses or explanations have helped me understand my experience?\n- Which needs remain difficult to explain or get support for?\n- What was already present earlier in my life?\n- What changed after frightening or overwhelming experiences?\n- What does an ordinary week ask of me, and where does recovery fit?\n- What question would I like a clinician to explore next?\n\nYou can bring one example. “I can attend the appointment, but I lose the ability to follow questions after twenty minutes” gives someone a specific problem to investigate. The whole picture can begin with one part that has been missing.","A short reply can sound efficient to the person sending it and cold to the person receiving it. A pause can feel necessary on one side of a conversation and alarming on the other. You can both give an honest account of the same moment and discover that you were responding to different things.\n\nUnderstanding starts with noticing where your interpretation enters the account. What did you hear? What did you think it meant? What did you do because you thought that? These are connected questions, and each can have a different answer.\n\n### Four parts of an interaction\n\nSuppose somebody leaves during a conversation. There are at least four things to consider.\n\nThe observable event is that the person left. Their account may be that speech had become difficult to follow. The other person's interpretation may be that they wanted to avoid the issue. A judgment about what should happen next might concern how to arrange a return to the conversation.\n\nIf those parts collapse into “You left because you do not care,” an inference has become the explanation. The person who left may then spend the next conversation defending their feelings while the original concern remains unresolved. If everything collapses into “I was overwhelmed,” the person left with an urgent decision may struggle to explain the consequence. A fuller account needs room for both experiences and the practical problem.\n\n### Mentalisation and curiosity\n\nMentalisation means considering the thoughts, feelings, intentions and beliefs that may help explain behaviour, including your own. It involves holding an understanding open to revision. In clinical work, the mentalising stance includes respect for how much of another person's experience remains uncertain to the observer. [Bateman and Fonagy on mentalisation-based treatment](https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/1489633/3/Fonagy_MBT%20chapter%20for%20Kaplan%20and%20Sadock%20complete%20071015.pdf)\n\nYou can practise that stance with a small change in language. “I thought you wanted me to handle it alone. Is that what you meant?” gives the other person something they can answer. “You always leave everything to me” may describe a real concern about workload, but it also asks them to respond to an entire pattern at once.\n\nCuriosity can include confidence about your own experience. You can say that being followed after asking for space frightened you. You can say that you had difficulty hearing the question. You can also ask what the other person remembers. Openness to their account does not require you to surrender yours.\n\n### When interpretation gets ahead of understanding\n\nThe more familiar a conflict becomes, the more quickly you may think you know where it is going. A particular tone arrives and you prepare for the next five minutes. Sometimes that expectation reflects a dependable pattern. Sometimes the expectation fills in details before the other person has had a chance to explain.\n\nAsk what you are drawing on. Is this a conclusion from repeated behaviour in this relationship? Is it connected with an earlier relationship? Is it a possibility that feels convincing because the moment is frightening? These questions can help you decide what information you need next.\n\nThere is a different problem when one person repeatedly claims authority over the other's experience. “You say you needed quiet, but I know you wanted to punish me” closes the question before the person can answer it. A conversation becomes difficult to repair when an explanation can never be corrected by the person it describes. We can address behaviour and consequences while acknowledging uncertainty about motives.\n\n### Communication has conditions\n\nClarity involves more than choosing simple words. It also concerns pace, timing, how much information arrives at once and what the person can use in that setting. A detailed explanation can be welcome at one time and impossible to follow at another.\n\nSome people understand a question more easily when they can read it. Some need to talk through their answer. Others need time alone before they know what their answer is. The same person may prefer different forms of communication for different subjects. Asking about these preferences gives you better information than assuming a diagnosis tells you how somebody communicates.\n\nWritten communication can help because the words remain available. It can also carry too much information or leave tone open to interpretation. A short message about one decision may work better than a long account of everything that went wrong. You can agree what belongs in writing and what needs another format.\n\n### Care can be expressed in several ways\n\nYou may feel cared for when somebody remembers a sensory preference, gives notice of a change or joins you in an interest. You may value practical help and still want verbal affection. Sharing quiet space may feel connecting to one person and distant to another.\n\nThe useful information is what each person experiences as care. “When you send me a message if you will be late, I can settle into the evening” explains why an action matters. “I like listening to your interests, and I need to know whether you want questions or company” helps make shared time easier to enjoy.\n\nAn intention to care deserves a way to reach the person receiving it. If the chosen form repeatedly feels overwhelming or leaves an important need unanswered, that is information to use. It can lead to a different action. Both people get to explain what helps them feel connected.\n\n### Acknowledging impact while checking meaning\n\nYou can begin with the part you know. “My reply was very short. I can understand why you were unsure what I meant. I was trying to answer before the meeting, and I want to finish the conversation tonight.” That account recognises what happened and offers information about intention and follow-through.\n\nYou can also ask for the consequence to be heard. “I understand that you were trying to help. When the plan changed without asking me, I had to rearrange the afternoon. Please check with me before making that commitment again.” You have described the impact and a future request without needing to settle how considerate the other person is.\n\n### Timing the question\n\nQuestions can become another source of input. If someone has just said they cannot follow a conversation, asking them to explain the experience in detail may make the situation harder. You can agree a later point to return, including how to handle decisions that cannot wait.\n\nIf you have a choice, ask for consent to begin. “Do you have room for one question about this morning?” makes the size of the request clearer. If the answer is no, the next useful question may concern a time or format, rather than the content itself.\n\n### A meaning check\n\nUse these prompts for a single interaction.\n\n- What could another person have observed?\n- What did I experience internally?\n- What meaning did I attach to the other person's behaviour?\n- What have they actually told me about their experience?\n- What impact still needs to be addressed?\n- What question or request could make the next step clearer?\n\nUseful wording can be brief.\n\n“What I heard was ____. Have I understood?”\n\n“I can explain what I meant, and I also want to hear what happened for you.”\n\n“I need more time to answer. Can I send you a message this evening?”\n\nThe words become useful when the other person has room to answer honestly, including when their answer changes your understanding.","Knowing how to do something and being able to do it at a particular moment are different parts of capacity. You might understand the task and struggle to begin. You might know what you want to say and lose access to the words. You might complete an activity today and need substantial recovery afterwards.\n\nWhen these differences are difficult to see, people often judge capacity by the outcome. The appointment was attended, so attending must have been manageable. Dinner appeared, so there must have been enough energy to make it. To understand what is sustainable, we also need to ask what it took and what followed.\n\n### The work inside a task\n\nAn everyday task can contain many activities. Moving an appointment may involve finding a message, comparing calendars, deciding which commitment can move, making a call, answering unexpected questions and remembering to tell other people. Some parts may be easy while another makes the task difficult to complete.\n\nExecutive function is a term for processes involved in organising and directing activity, such as beginning, holding information in mind, changing course and keeping track of what comes next. Asking which part is difficult can reveal a useful adjustment. A reminder helps with remembering. It may do little for a task whose next step remains unclear.\n\nThe environment adds work. A person following speech in noise must deal with both the message and what competes with it. Switching away from an unfinished activity adds another demand. Being hungry, in pain or short of sleep can further change what is available. These are questions to investigate in a particular person's circumstances.\n\n### Visible competence and hidden effort\n\nPeople can develop elaborate ways to get through situations that remain difficult. They may rehearse conversation, copy other people's expressions or hide movements that help them cope. Research on autistic camouflaging includes descriptions of these strategies and their reported costs. Looking composed can conceal a great deal of work. [Hull and colleagues on social camouflaging](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/pmid/28527095/)\n\nIn a relationship, the cost may appear after the public demand ends. A person can speak throughout a meeting and have little speech available at home. Their partner may reasonably wonder why conversation was possible for everyone else. The answer needs to include the whole day, including the possibility that they spent what they had before getting home.\n\nThis gives both people a planning problem. The person who needs recovery requires access to it. The person who has been waiting for contact or a decision also needs a workable arrangement. Knowing when recovery is likely to be needed can help them plan before either is carrying an urgent, unexplained gap.\n\n### Recognising overload\n\nYour early signs may be easier to notice in retrospect. Words become harder to follow. Repeated questions feel increasingly sharp. You lose your place in a familiar sequence. You become very still, move more, repeat yourself or struggle to choose between options. Other people may notice a change in tone before they understand what it means.\n\nIt can help to describe the earliest sign you can reliably recognise. “I am reading the same sentence repeatedly” may be more useful than waiting until you can confidently name a state. A partner can ask what support you want, or notice that a pre-agreed handover is due. Their observation should remain open to correction.\n\n### Shutdowns and meltdowns\n\nAutistic people and those supporting them use the terms shutdown and meltdown to describe experiences of overwhelming distress. During a meltdown, distress may become visibly intense through crying, shouting, movement or other behaviour. During a shutdown, a person may become less responsive, struggle to speak or find it difficult to act. Individual experiences and sequences vary. Both can require time and reduced demands. [NHS information on supporting an autistic person](https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/brain-nerves-and-spinal-cord/autism/supporting-an-autistic-person/)\n\nAppearance alone leaves questions unanswered. Quietness may be exhaustion, a decision to pause, a shutdown, fear or something else. Agitation can occur with panic, pain and other difficulties. If an experience is new, markedly different or medically concerning, assessment may be needed to understand it.\n\nSupport begins with immediate safety and what is already known about the person. Reduce the number of people speaking. Make it easier to leave a difficult setting where possible. Offer a simple choice rather than a series of questions. Ask before touching. A familiar calming activity may help one person and be unwelcome to another. Urgent danger or a possible medical emergency requires appropriate immediate help.\n\nThe period afterwards also deserves care. Someone may look calmer before they can discuss what happened, resume a demanding task or make a plan. A check-in can ask about water, food, pain, quiet or practical help. A later conversation can address consequences and prevention when there is more capacity to participate.\n\n### Recovery across days and weeks\n\nIf demand repeatedly exceeds recovery, a short break may cease to be enough. In research on autistic burnout, participants have described prolonged exhaustion, reduced access to skills and difficulty tolerating demands. These accounts bring attention to a pattern across life, beyond a single difficult episode. [Qualitative study of autistic burnout](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13005893/)\n\nPersistent change deserves a fuller look. Depression, sleep difficulties, pain and other health problems can also affect energy and functioning, and several difficulties can coexist. Keeping track of what has changed, how long it has lasted and what helps gives a clinician more to work with.\n\nAt home, ask what the current pattern depends on. Does one person's rest require another to postpone theirs indefinitely? Are essential jobs being carried by somebody who has already run out of room? Is recovery repeatedly interrupted by the work of arranging it? These questions make a support plan more realistic.\n\n### Planning while you have room to plan\n\nA useful plan is short enough to use during difficulty. You might agree that a particular word or message means the conversation needs to pause. You might identify one person who takes over an essential task, or a backup who can be contacted. If children need care, name how that care continues.\n\nInclude a check-in rather than an exact prediction of recovery. “I will message at seven to say what I can do next” can be more feasible than promising a full conversation at seven. The agreement can also specify what happens if the message does not arrive, so the other person can act without repeated chasing.\n\n### A capacity and recovery plan\n\n- My earliest recognisable signs are ____.\n- The input or demand I most need reduced is ____.\n- A short signal I can use is ____.\n- Support I usually welcome is ____.\n- Support that needs my consent each time is ____.\n- Essential care or decisions will be handled by ____.\n- We will check in using ____.\n- If the first plan is unavailable, our fallback is ____.\n\nTry the plan on an ordinary day and review what was difficult. A plan that requires more communication than you can manage can be made smaller. The useful measure is whether you can reach support earlier and whether the other people affected know what they can do next.","You bring a history to closeness. You have learnt what happens when you need help, disagree, ask for space or depend on another person. Some lessons are reassuring. Others teach you to anticipate disbelief, intrusion, rejection or an abrupt change in what is expected of you.\n\nNeurodivergence is part of the person having those experiences. Sensory preferences, attention, communication and ways of processing information influence what situations are like from the inside. The environment and other people's responses can then influence what you learn to expect. Over time, a practical need and a learned fear may become closely connected.\n\n### The neurodivergent skeleton\n\nThe phrase neurodivergent skeleton is a metaphor for the developmental structure within which a life takes shape. It invites us to ask how a person's existing characteristics meet their experiences. In this companion it is a way to organise questions about a life, with room for development and reciprocal influence.\n\nSomeone who needs clear plans may find them useful for organising attention and anticipating transitions. They may also have a history of being left to manage sudden changes alone. In adulthood, an unanswered planning question can carry practical uncertainty and a fear about being abandoned with the consequences. Explaining one part leaves another part worth exploring.\n\nAnother person may need solitude after prolonged conversation. They may also have learnt that asking for solitude leads to criticism. Leaving quickly can become a way to obtain relief before a negotiation starts. A partner who sees only the departure may have little idea what preceded it.\n\nUnderstanding these layers gives us several possible places to intervene. We can make the plan clearer. We can respect a request for recovery. We can investigate an earlier fear. We can also examine how a present relationship repeatedly responds to these needs.\n\n### Attachment in adult relationships\n\nAttachment concerns how we seek and experience closeness, support and dependable connection. In adult romantic attachment research, anxiety and avoidance are commonly studied as dimensions. Attachment anxiety concerns worries about a partner's availability or commitment. Avoidance concerns discomfort with dependence and emotional closeness. Researchers also examine orientations within particular relationships. [Fraley and colleagues on attachment across relationships](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21443364/)\n\nYou may encounter the labels secure, anxious, avoidant and fearful or disorganised in popular discussions. They can help start a conversation about patterns, but their use across research and clinical settings varies. For reflection here, it is more useful to describe what happens, when and with whom than to settle your identity into a category.\n\nYou might feel comfortable asking one person for help and very reluctant to ask another. You might seek reassurance in a relationship that has become unpredictable while feeling settled elsewhere. Patterns across several experiences can reveal something about earlier learning, the present relationship and the circumstances in which support becomes difficult to seek.\n\n### What protection tries to accomplish\n\nA protective response often makes sense when we ask what relief it offers. Repeated questions may briefly reduce uncertainty. Withdrawal may end a difficult exchange. Agreeing immediately may reduce the risk of conflict. Explaining at length may offer hope that being fully understood will make disagreement less dangerous.\n\nThen ask what follows. Does repeated reassurance leave either person more able to act? Does withdrawal provide recovery followed by a return, or do important concerns remain unresolved? Does agreement last once the immediate pressure has passed? A response can offer short-term relief and create a problem that returns later.\n\nThis is where the original book's language of protectors connects with the companion. We can explore what a response is trying to secure while also observing its effect on other people. Insight becomes useful when it helps identify an action, an accommodation or a boundary that changes the recurring situation.\n\n### Recovery, distance and the meaning of silence\n\nA need for quiet can be a straightforward need for quiet. It can also exist alongside worries about closeness or conflict. To understand a particular withdrawal, consider what happens before it, what the person reports and whether other conditions change it.\n\nIf written communication makes it possible to address the concern, that tells you something about access. If the person is comfortable discussing a difficult topic after recovery, that gives you another piece of information. If every available time and format closes down, the other person has an unresolved relational problem to address. Several explanations may remain possible while the impact becomes clear.\n\nThe same care is needed with reassurance. Asking when a partner will return may concern transport, care, connection or fear. A repeated history of broken commitments changes what a promise means. Ask what information is needed and whether the person giving it has been dependable. Earlier attachment experiences and present conduct both belong in the account.\n\n### Rejection sensitivity and uncertainty\n\nSome people describe rejection as arriving with unusual speed and force. A brief reply can feel like the beginning of a loss. An ambiguous expression can occupy attention long after the conversation ends. Rejection sensitivity is useful language for describing that experience and asking what happens around it.\n\nYou can explore the sequence without assuming that every interpretation is accurate. What was the cue? What did you think it meant? What changed in your body or attention? What action followed? What did you learn later? The sequence can reveal a moment where clarification, time or support would be useful.\n\nIt can also reveal accurate information about the relationship. If a person repeatedly humiliates you or threatens closeness when you disagree, fear has a present context. Understanding earlier experiences should make room for seeing what is happening now. A person's ability to tolerate a situation tells us little, by itself, about whether that situation is safe or fair.\n\n### Two people's histories in one relationship\n\nWhen one person seeks contact urgently and another withdraws urgently, each response can increase the pressure on the other. The person seeking contact encounters more distance. The person needing distance encounters more pursuit. Both may be trying to obtain relief, and each still has responsibility for their conduct.\n\nA different arrangement needs practical detail. How can one person get space? What information allows the other to make decisions? When can the subject be addressed? What can each person genuinely offer? These questions become easier when you can discuss them outside the moment of greatest pressure.\n\nThey also require freedom to answer. A negotiated plan has little value if a person is punished for using it or cannot decline a form of contact. Where fear or coercion affects that freedom, support may need to happen separately and with attention to safety. The conditions of the relationship matter to what work can be done together.\n\n### A protection and connection map\n\nChoose one recurring response that you want to understand.\n\n- The situation that sets it off is ____.\n- What I notice first is ____.\n- What I fear or expect is ____.\n- My response is ____.\n- The immediate relief it offers is ____.\n- What it costs me or another person is ____.\n- A practical need involved may be ____.\n- An earlier experience connected with it may be ____.\n- A change in the present relationship that would help is ____.\n\nYou can stop with an unanswered question. “I do not yet know why this feels so frightening” is useful information. It leaves room to investigate, while identifying the support or limit needed today. A clearer understanding of your history can develop alongside a more workable response in the present.","You can want to do something and feel an increasing resistance as soon as it becomes an expectation. An activity you have looked forward to can become difficult when someone tells you it is time to begin. A reminder can add pressure to a task you already know needs doing. You may find yourself unable to move towards something you have chosen for yourself.\n\nFrom the outside, the sequence can be confusing. Someone has asked for an ordinary thing. Perhaps they have asked politely, offered help or explained why it matters. They may feel that the next reasonable step is to ask again. Meanwhile, the person receiving the request may be experiencing each repetition as another obstacle to acting.\n\nUnderstanding the difficulty requires looking at the demand itself, the circumstances around it and what the person experiences when it arrives. Within a family, we also need to consider what happens to everyone else while the task remains unresolved.\n\n### What counts as a demand?\n\nA demand can be an instruction, a question, a deadline or an implied expectation. It can come from another person, an institution or your own plans. A message waiting for a reply can feel demanding before anyone sends a reminder. Hunger can create an urgent need to prepare food at the point when preparing food already feels difficult.\n\nThe National Autistic Society describes marked demand avoidance as an experience reported by and observed in some autistic people. It includes resistance to external expectations and internal demands, including activities that would otherwise be welcome. Its guidance also describes substantial uncertainty about definitions, prevalence, causes and support. [National Autistic Society guidance on demand avoidance](https://www.autism.org.uk/advice-and-guidance/behaviour/demand-avoidance)\n\nThese uncertainties make a detailed account of the individual especially valuable. “Getting ready is difficult” is a starting point. “I can get dressed until someone starts watching the clock and reminding me” gives us a more specific sequence to explore.\n\n### Understanding the PDA language\n\nPathological demand avoidance, usually shortened to PDA, is a term used for a proposed profile of pronounced demand avoidance. You may also hear people describe a persistent drive for autonomy. Some find these descriptions helpful in recognising their experience or seeking support. Terminology and recognition vary across services and communities.\n\nPDA has no standalone diagnosis in the major diagnostic manuals. Some assessments describe an autistic person's demand-avoidant profile. Early research included the development of a parent-report questionnaire intended to measure these characteristics. That work provides one way of studying a reported pattern, alongside continuing questions about its boundaries and explanation. [Preliminary study developing the Extreme Demand Avoidance Questionnaire](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24117718/)\n\nAn assessment can ask what happens, how long it has been happening and which circumstances change it. You can describe substantial distress and support needs while questions about terminology remain open. A useful description should help people decide what to investigate and what to make more accessible.\n\n### Several difficulties can meet in one task\n\nTake the instruction to leave the house. A person may be finding it difficult to stop an absorbing activity. They may have trouble sequencing what to do next. Their shoes may hurt, the journey may be uncertain or the destination may contain a frightening experience. They may also describe the expectation itself as creating an intense loss of autonomy.\n\nEach possibility leads to a different question. Is the first step clear? Is the transition too abrupt? Does something physically hurt? What is expected at the destination? What happens if the person has more control over the method or timing? What do they say about the pressure to comply?\n\nThere can also be a straightforward disagreement. A person may understand the request and believe it is unfair, unnecessary or something they do not consent to. Being able to explore distress includes allowing someone to explain their objection. Family members can have different views about what should happen next.\n\nSeveral contributors may be present together. Someone who struggles to begin a task may also anticipate criticism because previous difficulties led to arguments. By the time another reminder arrives, the task and the expected response to failure may be difficult to separate.\n\n### Where trauma enters the picture\n\nIf earlier demands were accompanied by punishment, humiliation or loss of control, later requests may carry associations with those experiences. A particular tone, an adult standing in a doorway or the feeling of being watched can become part of what the person responds to.\n\nDemand-related distress may also be reported without an identified trauma explaining its onset. It is worth exploring the developmental history and the effects of later experiences separately. Ask what was present early, what changed after particular events and what the person has learnt to expect from asking for relief.\n\nFor families, the immediate question is what happens when distress is repeatedly met with more pressure. Does the person become more able to participate? Do they become frightened, silent or increasingly resistant? Does a similar activity go differently in another setting? Following the sequence can reveal an adjustment to try without requiring a final account of every cause.\n\n### The pressure can build on both sides\n\nThe person making a request also has a context. They may be managing a work deadline, transport, medication or another child's care. They may have their own difficulty tolerating uncertainty. Being unable to get an answer can leave them carrying a decision whose consequences affect the whole family.\n\nAs the deadline approaches, they may speak more quickly, offer more reasons and reduce the available choices. The other person may then find it even harder to respond. Both can end up concentrating on the other's behaviour while losing sight of what is making the situation difficult.\n\nIt helps to identify what is fixed and what remains flexible. A departure time may be fixed while the order of getting ready can change. An essential appointment may allow a different entrance, a written explanation or support from another adult. A household task may need ownership while its exact timing is negotiable.\n\n### Meaningful choice and clear information\n\nA choice is easier to trust when the options are real. If only one outcome is available, clarity about that constraint can be more useful than offering alternatives that will later be withdrawn. There may still be choices about who helps, where to begin or how information is communicated.\n\nSome people prefer an invitation or indirect wording. Others need a direct explanation because working out an implied expectation adds effort. You can ask what language helps when the person has room to answer. Notice whether a change actually reduces distress and improves access over time.\n\n“The appointment is at ten. We can go in through the quieter entrance. Would you like the plan written down?” gives information and offers an adjustment. Whether it helps depends on what is difficult for that person. The words deserve to be reviewed alongside the setting and the practical support available.\n\n### When important needs are at stake\n\nFood, health, education and care can make demand avoidance especially difficult to navigate. A child may need support with something that remains distressing. An adult may be struggling with an essential task while another adult has limited room to take it over.\n\nBegin by identifying the actual need. Does the task have to happen in this form? What are the consequences of delay? Can the person meet the underlying need another way? Where distress affects nutrition, hydration, medication or immediate safety, timely help from an appropriate professional may be needed to plan a safe response.\n\nParents need support with these decisions. They may be trying to protect one child's autonomy while meeting another child's urgent needs. They may be explaining the same difficulty to several services with different expectations. Their own exhaustion belongs in the account of what the family needs. A plan should specify where help comes from when the available adults cannot sustain it alone.\n\n### Review what happens afterwards\n\nWhen an activity goes better, ask what changed. Perhaps there was more time, less watching, a familiar person or a clearer first step. Perhaps the person could decide when to start. One successful attempt gives you something to explore again under comparable conditions.\n\nWhen an attempt remains difficult, review the conditions before increasing the pressure. Was the proposed adjustment relevant? Were several demands still arriving together? Could the person explain what helped? Were the adults able to follow the plan? These questions can make the next attempt more informed.\n\n### A demand review\n\nChoose one recurring demand that has become difficult for your household.\n\n- The underlying need or responsibility is ____.\n- The part that appears most difficult is ____.\n- What the person says about it is ____.\n- The circumstances that increase pressure are ____.\n- What is fixed is ____.\n- What can change about timing, method, environment or support is ____.\n- A meaningful choice we can offer is ____.\n- If the first arrangement fails, we will ____.\n- The outside support we need is ____.\n\nKeep the review small enough to use. Changing one recurring encounter can give everyone better information about what makes participation possible. It can also reveal where a family needs more help than a change in wording can provide.","One person needs to talk in order to organise their thoughts. Another needs quiet before they can find theirs. One wants to know exactly what will happen tomorrow. Another needs time before they can commit. A child wants closeness at the point when a parent has reached their limit for touch.\n\nThese moments ask more of a family than recognising that each need is real. People need an arrangement they can live with. Sometimes both needs can be met through a change in timing or method. Sometimes a practical constraint means one need has to wait, and the person waiting needs enough information and support to manage that.\n\nThe work begins with describing each need clearly enough to see where they meet.\n\n### Name the need and the proposed response\n\n“I need you to answer me now” contains a need and a particular way of trying to meet it. The need may concern reassurance, a decision or an urgent care arrangement. Identifying which one is involved creates more possibilities.\n\nSimilarly, “I need you to leave me alone” may concern quiet, freedom from questions, physical space or a fear of being pressed into agreement. A short explanation, if the person can offer one, can help another adult respond. It can also be agreed in advance so nobody has to produce a detailed account while overloaded.\n\nTry asking, “What do you need to be able to do next?” The answer may be surprisingly practical. One person needs a decision about transport. Another needs the questions to arrive in writing. Those needs can sometimes be met together.\n\n### Understanding the interaction\n\nThe double empathy perspective draws attention to how people with different experiences can misunderstand each other. An experimental study of information transfer found that autistic participants shared information effectively with other autistic participants, while mixed autistic and non-autistic groups lost more detail and reported lower rapport. That finding invites us to examine the interaction and its context. The study concerned a structured task, which is one part of the much wider experience of communication. [Crompton and colleagues' study of information transfer](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/1362361320919286)\n\nWithin a family, sharing a diagnosis can offer recognition without answering every question about what another person needs. Two autistic partners may have very different sensory preferences, processing rhythms and histories. Each can be trying to help in the way that would help them.\n\nQuiet company may feel comforting to one person and like a demand to interact to another. Detailed information may reduce one person's uncertainty while overwhelming the person receiving it. A useful question is, “What is this like for you?” The answer may change the help being offered.\n\n### Patterns of pursuit and withdrawal\n\nWhen distance feels frightening, a person may seek more contact, explanation or reassurance. When contact feels overwhelming, another may withdraw. The increased pursuit can intensify the need to leave, and the increased distance can intensify the wish to pursue.\n\nYou can notice this pattern through what happens between you. Who speaks again? Who moves away? What does each person expect will happen if they stop doing their part? These questions are often more informative than deciding which attachment label each person belongs to.\n\nSome families experience a different pattern. Both adults may retreat, leaving practical matters unresolved. Both may seek repeated confirmation until conversation becomes exhausting. People may also change roles across subjects or circumstances. A description should remain close enough to experience that either person can correct it.\n\n### Co-regulation needs consent\n\nCo-regulation is a way of describing how people influence one another's state through interaction. A welcome voice, familiar routine or quiet shared activity may help someone settle. For another person, the most helpful action may be to reduce contact and make space for their own recovery.\n\nAsk what is wanted. “Would you like company, quiet or a practical hand?” offers several forms of support without presuming touch or conversation. A person can decline. Their preference may change on another occasion.\n\nThe person offering support also has limits. They may be able to bring water and take over a task while having little capacity to listen. They may need recovery themselves. An agreement can include these limits so support does not depend on one person repeatedly overriding their own needs.\n\n### A pause needs a practical shape\n\nA pause can reduce pressure by answering a few questions before a difficult conversation begins. How will either person signal that they need it? What communication remains welcome? What happens to a decision that cannot wait? How will they know whether a later conversation is possible?\n\nChoose a signal that is available during difficulty. It might be a word, gesture or short message. Consider what happens if speech becomes hard to access. Make the action following the signal clear enough that it does not require another negotiation.\n\n“I need quiet. I will message at eight about when I can talk” offers a check-in. The person receiving it can decide what they can do until eight. If there is an essential question about a child's care, the agreement should explain how that question gets answered.\n\n### When the check-in is missed\n\nRecovery can take longer than expected. A missed message can also leave someone worried or unable to make a plan. Include a fallback for both possibilities.\n\nPerhaps the person taking space can send a brief update asking for more time. Perhaps the other person proceeds with a previously agreed arrangement after a particular point. If neither has capacity, a backup person or service may be needed. The appropriate arrangement depends on the household and the responsibilities involved.\n\nLater, review what happened. Was the original promise feasible? Was the message too complicated? Did someone forget, become less able to communicate or choose to avoid the issue? A repeated difficulty needs an adjustment and follow-through. Both people need confidence that the concern can eventually be addressed.\n\n### Returning without restarting the whole argument\n\nCheck whether both people can participate and what each can manage. A return might begin with one question, a short written account or agreement about the next practical step. A difficult subject can be divided into several conversations.\n\nBe clear about what remains unresolved. Reconnecting through a shared meal or quiet company can be welcome, and the original concern may still need attention. It helps to name a route back to it so one person's relief does not become the other person's uncertainty about whether they can raise it again.\n\nThe aim of a return is participation. Each person needs room to explain, listen, disagree and make choices. If either feels unable to do those things freely, that information matters to the kind of support required.\n\n### Children and essential care\n\nA family cannot always arrange a pause around the adults' preferred timing. Children may need food, supervision, reassurance or help with a transition. Those needs require a concrete handover. “I need a break” can be recognised alongside a plan for who is taking responsibility next.\n\nAgree what the children need to know in language they can use. They may need to know where a parent is, which adult can help and whether a familiar routine will continue. Adults can provide that information without asking the child to explain or soothe the adults' distress.\n\nIf both adults are reaching their limits, review the day's demands and the backup available. Some tasks may be postponed. Some care may require outside assistance. The children also need places to recover and ways to communicate their own limits. A household plan becomes more realistic when it includes each person rather than treating one member's capacity as permanently available.\n\n### A pause, support and return agreement\n\nYou can work through these questions separately before comparing answers.\n\n- How do I signal that I need the conversation to pause?\n- What kind of contact is welcome during that time?\n- What can I realistically communicate while recovering?\n- What information does the other person need in order to act?\n- Who handles essential care and urgent decisions?\n- When and how will we check in?\n- What is the fallback if that check-in is missed?\n- How do we return to the original concern?\n- What support is needed if we both have limited capacity?\n\nReview the agreement after using it. Notice whether each person could actually use the signal, whether practical needs were met and whether the subject could be resumed. Those ordinary details show where the arrangement is working and where it needs more support.","A person becomes quiet, repeats a question or insists that a plan stay the same. You can observe each of these behaviours. Understanding what is happening requires more information.\n\nPerhaps quietness provides relief from sound. Perhaps speaking feels risky because earlier attempts led to criticism. Perhaps a person is choosing to end an exchange they find unfair. More than one process may be involved, and the other people present may experience consequences that also need attention.\n\nThe challenge is to remain curious enough to investigate while being clear enough to respond to what is happening.\n\n### What the behaviour tells us\n\nAn observation describes an event. An explanation connects that event with a possible process. A diagnosis draws on a wider pattern, relevant criteria and an assessment of the person's history and functioning. Each involves a different amount and kind of information.\n\n“They asked whether I would return five times” describes something another person might verify. “They were afraid I would leave them” adds a possible explanation. The person may confirm it, offer another account or be unsure. There may also have been a practical reason the answer mattered, such as whether they could leave for work.\n\nSeparating these parts helps keep a conversation usable. A family member can describe the repeated question, explain its impact and ask what information was missing. A clinician can explore a broader history if the pattern raises diagnostic questions.\n\n### Similarity and co-occurrence\n\nNeurodevelopmental conditions, trauma-related difficulties and personality disorders can involve experiences that resemble one another from the outside. Assessment needs to consider whether a feature belongs to one condition, reflects several processes or calls for another explanation.\n\nA study comparing autistic women and people assigned female at birth with a BPD group examined both overlapping and differentiating characteristics. Its design compared selected groups rather than establishing how often an unrecognised condition occurs in everyone given either diagnosis. Such work supports asking more detailed questions about similarities and differences. [Study comparing autism and BPD characteristics](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41928549/)\n\nCo-occurrence is another possibility. A large Swedish register study found that recorded ADHD and BPD diagnoses were associated within individuals and across families. That finding gives reason to consider both where appropriate. It cannot tell a partner which process explains a particular argument or establish the full diagnostic picture of someone outside the study. [Swedish register study of ADHD and BPD](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7815504/)\n\nThe question remains practical. What needs assessment, what support is available and what has an existing account left unexplored?\n\n### When one diagnosis takes up the whole picture\n\nDiagnostic overshadowing describes a problem in which a recognised condition dominates interpretation and another difficulty receives inadequate attention. Someone's distress may be repeatedly attributed to a familiar diagnosis while pain, sensory needs, a new mental health difficulty or a change in circumstances goes unexplored.\n\nYou might recognise this when the explanation stays the same despite important new information. A person describes difficulty processing speech, and the conversation returns to willingness to engage. They report a longstanding need for predictability, and the account remains limited to their response to a recent relationship event.\n\nAn existing diagnosis may still be useful. A woman can need treatment for BPD and an assessment of previously unrecognised autism or ADHD. Sensory access may make treatment easier to use. Treatment may help with difficulties that persist even in a more accessible setting. Care can investigate both questions and review what changes.\n\n### Giving BPD and NPD their own consideration\n\nBorderline personality disorder and narcissistic personality disorder are distinct clinical questions. Research about overlap involving BPD needs to be considered within that scope. A family member deserves particular care before a diagnosis becomes the explanation for every disagreement.\n\nThe term narcissist is often used broadly in everyday accounts of hurtful behaviour. If someone repeatedly belittles another person, overrides their choices or dismisses the effects of their conduct, those actions can be described directly. An assessment of NPD would ask about an enduring pattern across the person's functioning, using substantially more information than one family interaction provides.\n\nThe same distinction helps when somebody has an authentic need for predictability and responds to a change by humiliating a partner. The household can investigate what would make changes easier to manage. The partner can also name the humiliation, its effect and what they will do if it happens again. Both questions deserve a response.\n\n### Ask about timing and context\n\nDevelopmental history can reveal whether a difficulty was present early. The timing of trauma can reveal changes after particular experiences. Current circumstances can reveal why a previously workable arrangement has become difficult. These histories can be explored together without forcing every part into a single explanation.\n\nAsk about settings. Does the difficulty occur in a noisy room and ease somewhere quieter? Does it appear mainly around a particular person or subject? What happens before and after it? Are there situations in which the person has the support they need and the same pattern persists?\n\nThese questions require care. A difference between settings can reflect different demands, different relationships or the work of concealing difficulty. Someone functioning at school or work may need considerable recovery afterwards. One observation becomes more informative when it is placed beside the person's account and several other occasions.\n\n### What accommodation can teach us\n\nA change in conditions can provide useful information. If written questions make participation easier, that gives a reason to keep written questions available. If a predictable plan reduces repeated checking, the plan is addressing something relevant. If a difficult pattern continues, it may be worth exploring other contributors as well.\n\nAccommodations should address the needs already described and remain open to review. Their effects can then help a family and professionals understand what else is needed. A change may help partly, help at some times or require several attempts before it is usable.\n\nNotice the cost of an adjustment too. If one adult handles all changes so another can avoid uncertainty, the arrangement may become difficult to sustain. You can ask what support helps each person participate and which responsibilities require another source of help.\n\n### Observing without putting family members on trial\n\nAn observation record is most useful when it is brief, specific and directed towards a question. Record what happened, relevant conditions, what the person reported and what followed. It should be possible to include uncertainty and an occasion that challenges your first interpretation.\n\nWhere feasible, let people contribute their own account. A child might find it easier to draw, point to a choice or explain later in a quiet setting. A partner may want to write separately before comparing notes. Choose a form that supports participation and takes privacy seriously.\n\nIf joint recording feels intimidating or increases fear, consider whether a private account for an appropriate professional would be more useful. The purpose of gathering information matters. Understanding is easier when people have freedom to correct what has been written about them.\n\n### What to take to an assessment\n\nBring the question you want help answering, together with a few relevant examples. Include what was present early, what has changed, what support has been tried and what the difficulty means for daily life. Records can help, and an assessor can explain what is possible when information is incomplete.\n\nIt may be useful to ask how the assessment will consider co-occurring conditions, trauma history and sensory or communication needs. You can also ask how feedback will be explained and what support follows from the findings. A fuller account should help make decisions about care and access.\n\nMeanwhile, a family still has the present situation to manage. A need for quiet can be discussed. A frightening interaction can be addressed. Essential care can be planned. Uncertainty about cause can sit alongside a clear account of impact and the next practical step.\n\n### An observation record\n\nChoose a repeated difficulty you want to understand.\n\n- What happened, in observable terms?\n- What was happening immediately before it?\n- What demands, sensory conditions or changes were present?\n- What does the person report about their experience?\n- What earlier history may be relevant?\n- What support or adjustment was tried?\n- What changed, and what remained difficult?\n- What was the impact on each person?\n- What question would benefit from professional assessment?\n- What needs to happen now, while that question remains open?\n\nReturn to the record after a little time. Look for what you have learnt, what you have had to revise and what support has become clearer. That is enough to move the inquiry forward while leaving room for a more complete understanding to develop.","You can recognise your limits, explain them clearly and still find that the household changes only when you can no longer continue. You might ask for quiet while you can still speak calmly, then spend another hour answering questions. You might request help before dinner and receive it once you are crying beside the stove.\n\nThere may be care in the response to your distress. The question is what would allow some of that care to arrive earlier. What happens to a need when it is expressed before the consequences become visible?\n\n### When endurance becomes part of the plan\n\nA family can become accustomed to one person finding a way. The school form gets completed, the appointment is rearranged and somebody remembers the missing item. Each finished task makes the arrangement look workable. The person doing it may know that the work consumed their recovery time or left something else unattended.\n\nOver time, their endurance can become an unspoken resource. Other plans depend on it. A request to change the arrangement can then seem surprising because the previous week was somehow managed. The effort required to manage it remains largely inside one person's experience.\n\nImproved regulation can make this harder to see. If you are learning to identify distress earlier or communicate it with less visible intensity, people may take the calmer presentation as evidence that the pressure has reduced. You may be doing more work to manage the same conditions. A household needs a way to respond to what you report while there is still room to prevent further difficulty.\n\n### What happens to an early request?\n\nConsider one recent occasion when somebody asked for help, rest or a change. Was the request heard? Did a decision follow? Did the person have to ask again, justify the need or prepare everything before another person could take over?\n\nThese details reveal how support becomes available. “Tell me if you need anything” can be a kind offer. It can also leave the work of noticing, deciding and arranging with the person who is already struggling. A more specific commitment gives them something they can rely on.\n\n“I will handle the school message this afternoon and tell you once it is resolved” includes the follow-through. It may be a small part of the week, but it removes a question from somebody else's attention. That can matter as much as the visible task itself.\n\n### Power in ordinary decisions\n\nPower appears in who can make plans, spend money, access transport, use time and decide what counts as reasonable. It also appears in whose account is accepted when the effects of an arrangement are difficult to see.\n\nOne adult may be able to announce a break while another has to negotiate for one. Paid work may have a recognised end point while care continues until everything is finished. One person's need for predictability may organise the day while another person's need for recovery waits for a convenient gap.\n\nLook at how those decisions are made. Can each person explain the cost of a plan? Can they disagree without being dismissed? Are the same kinds of constraints taken seriously for everyone? A fair conversation needs enough information about both needs and enough freedom for both people to influence the outcome.\n\nRespect for a person's judgment becomes visible when their account changes what happens. They can say that they need a different arrangement and expect a serious response. Approval for having coped well is welcome, but a family also needs to hear what made coping costly.\n\n### Who owns the work?\n\nThere is a difference between doing one step and taking responsibility for a task. A child's appointment may involve noticing the need, arranging the booking, preparing information, organising transport, attending, following recommendations and communicating with school. The appointment in the calendar is only one visible piece.\n\nIf one person continues to notice and coordinate every piece, handing over attendance may leave much of their load intact. It helps to define ownership. What will the person taking it on notice, decide and follow through? What information do they need? Which decisions still require consultation?\n\nOwnership can include support. A shared calendar, reminder or written sequence may help someone carry a responsibility. Agree who maintains those supports so the arrangement remains workable. The aim is for each person to know what they can depend on without having to monitor every step themselves.\n\n### Fairness when capacity differs\n\nFamily members have different capacities, and those capacities change. Sharing work fairly requires attention to the effort and consequences for each person. The same task can be relatively easy for one adult and consume most of another's available energy.\n\nIt may be sensible for one person to handle calls and another to organise written information. It may also be important for each to have access to activities they enjoy, relationships outside the household and meaningful rest. A division that uses strengths still needs review if it leaves one person permanently on duty.\n\nAsk what happens over a week. Who gets uninterrupted recovery? Who is available for the unexpected? Who loses sleep when a child needs support? Who makes up work afterwards? These questions show the arrangement in motion and help reveal costs that a list of chores can miss.\n\n### When the household has too little room\n\nTwo neurodivergent adults with trauma histories may each be doing their best to maintain employment, health and connection. Their children may need substantial support with transitions, communication, appointments and daily care. There can be days when the essential work exceeds the capacity of the people available.\n\nA useful review can make that shortfall visible. Which needs are urgent? Which expectations can be reduced? What support is missing from school, healthcare, employment or the wider network? Who can help find it? Guidance for autistic adults recognises that family members and carers can have support needs of their own, including respite and emergency planning. [NICE guidance on support for families and carers](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg142/evidence/full-guideline-186587677)\n\nAccessing help also takes work. Applications, calls and retelling the history can consume the energy the support is intended to protect. If possible, include help with obtaining help. Ask a service what it can coordinate, what information can be reused and how communication can be made accessible.\n\n### Change the ordinary day\n\nChoose a recurring point where pressure builds. Perhaps the transition home from school needs less conversation, or dinner needs an agreed simpler option. Perhaps the person returning from work needs recovery and the adult who has been caring all day needs a dependable handover. Specify what happens and when.\n\nTry one change that is small enough to carry out and substantial enough to affect the load. Review whether it helped the person it was meant to support and what it required of everyone else. If the change created a new difficulty, adjust it using that information.\n\nThe measure is what becomes possible in ordinary life. Can someone rest before they become overwhelmed? Does a school matter get resolved without repeated reminders? Can a parent attend their own appointment? These are concrete signs that the arrangement is changing.\n\n### A household load review\n\n- The essential work this week includes ____.\n- The person responsible for each task from beginning to end is ____.\n- The tasks that remain unowned or repeatedly return to one person are ____.\n- The earliest requests for help we tend to miss are ____.\n- Each person's dependable recovery time is ____.\n- A recurring demand we can reduce is ____.\n- Our fallback for an especially difficult day is ____.\n- Support that needs to come from outside the household is ____.\n- The change we will try first is ____.\n- We will review it on ____.\n\nYou can begin with one question if a full review would be too much. A reliable change to one part of the day gives the family something to build on and makes the remaining need easier to describe.","Understanding why something happened can bring relief. An interaction that felt incomprehensible may become easier to recognise when you understand overload, fear, shame or difficulty accessing words. You may feel more compassion for yourself or another person.\n\nThere is still an event to respond to. Somebody may have felt frightened, lost access to a decision or been left with more work than they could manage. Understanding can help shape what happens next. Repair needs to reach the people affected.\n\n### Four questions after harm\n\nWhat helps explain the action? What was its impact? What responsibility follows? What would repair require?\n\nThese questions allow a conversation to move between different parts of the experience. A person can describe the overload that preceded an outburst. Another can explain that the shouting frightened them. The discussion can then consider what support would make an earlier pause possible and what each person will do if the signs appear again.\n\nCapacity and intention matter to the response. The support required after an unexpected loss of capacity may differ from the response to a knowingly broken agreement. A repeated pattern also brings information about whether the current plan is sufficient and whether someone is following through on changes they can make.\n\nAccountability becomes more workable when it identifies an action. What can be acknowledged now? What needs assessment? What arrangement can change? What consequence remains even after an apology? A conversation can address these questions while some uncertainty about the event remains.\n\n### Describe the part you know\n\n“When you followed me after I asked for space, I felt frightened and found it harder to speak” gives an account of behaviour and impact. “When you left without telling me whether you would return, I could not make the transport decision” does the same.\n\nThe person hearing this can begin by acknowledging the event. They can offer their account without requiring the other person to withdraw theirs. Where memories differ, it may still be possible to identify a future arrangement both can use. Where there is a serious dispute about safety or events, independent support may be needed.\n\nAn explanation becomes useful when it helps the next step. “I was already struggling to follow speech” can lead to an earlier signal and a written option. “I was afraid the plan would fall apart” can lead to a fallback for urgent decisions. The change gives the explanation a practical role.\n\n### Words and follow-through\n\nAn apology can matter. It can show that a person has heard the impact and wants to take responsibility. The person affected may also need time before they know whether they can rely on what was said.\n\nLook for the conduct connected with the apology. Does the person stop when asked? Do they take ownership of the task they agreed to carry? Do they return to the concern after taking a pause? Do they acknowledge a missed commitment without making the other person pursue the issue again?\n\nPromises are easier to assess when they are specific and feasible. “I will do better” leaves several questions open. “I will send the update before pickup, and if I cannot, I will use our backup plan” gives the other person something observable. Review whether the plan addresses the original impact and whether it is actually being used.\n\n### Reconnection and repair\n\nAfter conflict, some people welcome quiet company, a cup of tea or a familiar activity. Others need solitude. A mutually welcome ritual can make returning to contact easier. Ask whether the other person wants it and leave room for a different answer on another day.\n\nThe original issue may still need a conversation or decision. You can agree that reconnection happens now and a particular concern will be addressed later. Make that route back clear enough that relief does not leave somebody wondering whether the subject has disappeared again.\n\nRepair may include acknowledging harm, replacing something damaged, taking over work, seeking treatment or changing a recurring arrangement. What matters depends on the event. The person affected can describe what would help, including when closeness is something they need time to decide about.\n\n### Requests, agreements and boundaries\n\nA request asks another person to do something. An agreement records what people have freely committed to do. A boundary describes the limits of your participation and the actions available to you when those limits are reached.\n\n“Please ask before touching me” is a request. “We will ask before offering a hug during distress” can be an agreement. “If I do not want touch, I will step away and ask for space” describes an action you may take. The appropriate action depends on what is safe and possible for you.\n\nBoundaries can concern sound, time, touch, money, privacy and the way a conversation proceeds. They can also concern access to your home, body or continued relationship. Give them a form that is clear enough to use. You may need help to communicate or act on a limit, especially if other people control resources you depend on.\n\n### When a limit is difficult to hear\n\nSomeone may feel rejected when you decline contact or ask for a pause. You can hear that feeling and offer information you can honestly give. “I need quiet. I can check in this evening” may reduce uncertainty. The other person still has to allow the space you have requested.\n\nThe same applies when you are the person waiting. Your distress deserves support, and another person's capacity and consent affect what support they can offer. An arrangement may include another trusted person, an individual coping plan or professional care. Consider what you can use when the person you most want is unavailable.\n\nThis is particularly important in families where both adults have substantial needs. Support becomes more sustainable when each person has options and when essential care has a fallback beyond whichever adult can still keep going.\n\n### When negotiation is affected by fear\n\nAn agreement needs freedom to disagree, decline and use the agreed limit. Threats, intimidation, control of resources or consequences for asking for space can restrict that freedom. In those circumstances, private specialist support and individual safety planning may be needed before attempting a shared process. Safety planning can be developed with a domestic and family violence service and tailored to the person's circumstances. [1800RESPECT information on safety planning](https://1800respect.org.au/help-and-support/safety-planning)\n\nYou do not have to settle a diagnosis or an account of motive before describing what frightens you. The practical questions concern your safety, available support and choices. A plan should take account of access needs, children, finances and the risks attached to particular actions.\n\n### Review the pattern\n\nThere may be progress and setbacks. Ask what changes after a setback. Is it acknowledged? Does the plan become more realistic? Is further support sought? Does the person affected have more freedom and less work over time?\n\nYou may discover that some parts of the relationship can change while others remain difficult. You may also decide that the changes offered are insufficient for continued closeness. The choice about your participation belongs in the process, alongside compassion for the other person's history and needs.\n\n### A repair and boundary plan\n\n- The behaviour we need to address is ____.\n- The impact, including practical consequences, was ____.\n- What each person can acknowledge is ____.\n- Support or assessment needed is ____.\n- The specific change each person agrees to make is ____.\n- The limit I need to communicate is ____.\n- The action available to me if it is crossed is ____.\n- The support I need to use that limit safely is ____.\n- We will review follow-through by looking for ____.\n\nA plan can begin with one dependable change. It becomes credible through experience, including what happens when somebody is tired, disappointed or asked to do something inconvenient.","Children live inside the arrangements adults create. They notice routines, changes in tone, who is available and what happens when they need something. They also bring their own sensory preferences, communication needs, fears, interests and ways of making sense of events.\n\nIn a family carrying substantial demands, a child may become very good at reading the room. They may wait to ask a question, offer comfort or take on a task without being asked. Understanding what they are carrying requires looking beyond whether the household has continued to function.\n\n### The child's day has a history too\n\nA difficult transition at home may follow hours of effort at school. A child may have spent the day managing noise, uncertainty, social demands or a setting in which asking for help feels hard. What becomes visible after pickup is one part of that day.\n\nAsk what information can come from the child and the people who support them. Notice which conditions make expression easier. They may want to talk while moving, write later, draw, use an existing communication aid or answer a small number of concrete questions. Give them time and avoid assuming that an immediate answer is the most complete one.\n\nBehaviour can guide an inquiry, but it leaves much to ask. Quietness may feel comfortable or reflect difficulty seeking help. A repeated question may concern predictability. A refusal may concern pain, fear, sensory discomfort or a preference that deserves to be heard. Each child's account adds information that adults may otherwise miss.\n\n### When children begin managing adults\n\nFamily members naturally care about each other. A child can offer affection, help with an appropriate task and take pride in contributing. The arrangement deserves review when they become responsible for keeping an adult emotionally steady or protecting the adult from information the child needs to share.\n\nThey may reassure a parent that the other parent is wrong, carry a message because adults cannot communicate directly or conceal a positive experience because it seems likely to hurt somebody. They may decide that being easy to care for is the best way to keep the family settled.\n\nAsk whether the child can decline, need help or have a different view. Can they be upset without having to comfort the adult afterwards? Can they say that an arrangement is difficult without being asked to defend one parent? Those freedoms help reveal what the child has come to understand as their job.\n\n### Loyalty pressure can arrive quietly\n\nA child can feel caught between adults through repeated comments, questions or emotional reactions. “You know how much I have done” may invite them to reassure a parent. “Tell your other parent I already asked” may give them responsibility for an adult exchange. A disappointed response to their affection for someone else may influence what they feel able to show next time.\n\nResearch interviews with children and young people in separated families describe experiences of pressure to take sides, being caught between parents and wanting their own needs and views to be heard. These accounts give adults reason to attend closely to the child's experience of an arrangement. [Australian Institute of Family Studies research on children and young people in separated families](https://aifs.gov.au/all-research/research-reports/children-and-young-people-separated-families-family-law-system)\n\nSimilar questions can be asked in a shared household. Who does the child approach when adults disagree? Do they expect to deliver an answer between them? Have they learnt to hide part of their life to manage an adult's reaction? A parent can investigate these questions without first deciding whether the pressure was deliberate.\n\n### Listen carefully when a child wants distance\n\nA child may struggle with a transition, a particular sensory environment or an interaction they find frightening. They may also want more time with one person, be responding to a developmental change or have experienced harm in the relationship. A request for distance needs enough room for these possibilities to be explored.\n\nAsk concrete, open questions that allow the child to describe what happens. “What is the hardest part of going?” may reveal something different from “Why do you not want to see them?” A trusted professional may be needed where there are concerns about safety or where the child finds it difficult to speak within the family.\n\nGive a child's report of harm appropriate attention and safeguarding. Decisions about contact or care should take account of their safety and the relevant professional or legal support. Their reluctance deserves investigation in its context, with space for their own experience rather than a predetermined account of adult motives.\n\n### Take the adult task back\n\nBegin with something specific. If a child has become the messenger, identify the adult communication route that will replace it. If they have been asked to explain one parent's choices to the other, tell them who will handle those questions. If they have taken responsibility for knowing everyone's schedule, make the information available in a form they can use without maintaining it themselves.\n\nYou can acknowledge a mistake simply. “I asked you to pass that message. I will send it myself next time.” Follow with the change. The child can respond in their own time, including by saying very little.\n\nWhen adult conflict has affected them, explain what they need to know. “You heard us arguing. We are taking a break and getting help with the problem. You can ask me about dinner or anything you need tonight.” Keep promises within what you know you can provide and make essential care predictable.\n\n### Parents need recovery and support\n\nNeurodivergent parents may need accessible communication, sensory adjustments, treatment and practical help alongside support for their children. Trauma can add difficulty around being judged, asking for help or dealing with services. A plan that ignores the parent's needs can become difficult to sustain.\n\nInclude what allows a parent to recover while care continues. Another adult may take over for a defined period. A familiar routine may make the handover easier for a child. Outside assistance may be essential. The adult arranging help may also need support with the calls, forms and coordination involved.\n\nChildren can be told that an adult needs rest without being made responsible for providing it. Explain who is available and what happens next. The child still needs room to require care, express disappointment and have questions of their own.\n\n### Repair that children can experience\n\nAn adult's explanation becomes more meaningful when the child's day changes. Their question gets answered without an argument. They can enjoy time with somebody without checking the parent's face afterwards. The adult returns when expected or gives an update. A routine accommodates their need for quiet.\n\nReview changes with the child in a way they can use. Ask what has become easier and what remains difficult. Take their answer seriously even when it differs from the adults' view of progress. Their experience is part of the evidence about whether the arrangement is helping.\n\n### A family responsibility check\n\n- What has the child begun noticing, arranging or emotionally managing?\n- Which parts are appropriate for their age, capacity and wishes?\n- Which responsibility belongs with adults or services?\n- Who will take it back, and how will the child know?\n- What information does the child need about daily care and changes?\n- How can they express discomfort, preference or fear accessibly?\n- Who else can listen and provide support?\n- What observable change will show that the adult task has moved back to adults?\n\nYou can return to these questions as children grow and circumstances change. A child's increasing ability to help gives the family something to appreciate. Their continuing need for support belongs in the same picture.","Understanding can change the questions you ask about your life. A difficulty you once described only as failure may now include a sensory barrier, an unrealistic workload or an earlier experience that still shapes what feels possible. A familiar relationship pattern may become easier to recognise before it gathers speed.\n\nThe next question concerns the life you want to make possible with that understanding. What would give your needs room? What support would help you carry your responsibilities? What would make closeness welcome and disagreement possible? The answers can begin with an ordinary day.\n\n### Choose one place to begin\n\nYou may have recognised several things across these chapters. Trying to change them all at once can add another demanding project to a crowded life. Choose one recurring difficulty whose improvement would make a meaningful difference.\n\nIt might be the transition home, the way school messages are handled or the point at which conversations become difficult to follow. It might be a question you want to take to a clinician. Describe what happens now and what a workable change would look like.\n\nMake the first step small enough to use. A single agreed handover may be more manageable than a complete household redesign. A brief written account may help you begin an assessment conversation. An earlier signal for a pause can give a relationship another way to respond. Review the actual effect before adding more.\n\n### Keep your own needs in the picture\n\nFamily life can direct attention towards whoever needs the most immediate help. Your own needs may become visible only when they prevent you from continuing to care for someone else. Bring them into the plan earlier if you can.\n\nThat includes health, sleep, sensory access, meaningful activity, relationships and time in which you are free from coordinating the next thing. Consider which needs can be met within the household and which require something beyond it. A plan can name an unmet need even when access to the support remains difficult.\n\nYou also get to have preferences. You may want shared interests, affection, privacy, novelty, familiar routines or more time alone. Practical survival can occupy so much of family life that these desires receive little attention. They offer information about what a more liveable arrangement could include.\n\n### Support and care that fit the person\n\nDifferent questions may need different kinds of professional support. Assessment can explore an unresolved diagnostic picture. Mental health care can address symptoms and patterns that remain distressing. Occupational or practical support can help investigate daily activities and access needs. Relationship or family work can address shared arrangements where participation is safe and freely chosen.\n\nAsk what the support is intended to help with, how it works and how progress will be reviewed. Discuss communication, pacing, sensory conditions and the support needed between appointments. NICE guidance recommends adapting psychological interventions for autistic adults with coexisting mental health difficulties, including a more concrete approach and greater use of written or visual information. [NICE guidance on adapting care for autistic adults](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg142/resources/full-guideline-pdf-186587677)\n\nAn accessible appointment can make it easier to show what is happening. You may want questions ahead of time, permission to write, regular breaks or a summary you can revisit. Explain the adjustment and what it helps you do. If a provider cannot offer it, ask what alternatives are available.\n\n### Questions to ask a provider\n\nYou can choose a few questions relevant to the help you are seeking.\n\n- What experience do you have supporting people with my needs and co-occurring conditions?\n- How will we agree the goals of this work?\n- How can communication, pace and the environment be adapted?\n- How will you consider trauma alongside neurodevelopmental history?\n- What can I do if I cannot answer a question in the appointment?\n- What work is expected between sessions, and how can we make it feasible?\n- How will we review whether the support is helping?\n- How do you handle concerns about consent, safety or power in relationship work?\n\nYou may need to raise a question more than once or ask someone to help you prepare. The discussion itself can give you information about whether you can explain your experience and influence the care you receive.\n\n### Change you can recognise\n\nLook at ordinary experiences over time. Can you ask for rest while you can still speak calmly? Does someone take ownership of work without requiring you to supervise it? Can your child explain a preference without being caught in adult disagreement? Does a paused conversation eventually return to the original concern?\n\nSome changes may be modest and still matter. A reliable update can reduce uncertainty. A quieter appointment can leave more capacity for the rest of the day. An adult communication route can remove a task a child had been carrying. Notice the benefit and the effort required to maintain it.\n\nProgress can also reveal what remains difficult. An adjustment may help communication while leaving an unequal workload intact. Better regulation may make it easier to name a need that is still being dismissed. Use what has improved to ask a more precise question about what has yet to change.\n\n### You can decide about closeness\n\nUnderstanding another person's history can deepen compassion and leave you with choices about the relationship. You can consider what contact you want, what conditions make it sustainable and what support you need to make decisions freely.\n\nSome relationships become more workable through dependable changes. Others continue to ask more than a person can carry or remain frightening despite repeated attempts to address the problem. Your account of that pattern matters. Individual support may help you consider options, practical constraints and safety.\n\nFamily arrangements can change while responsibilities for children's care continue. Those decisions may require professional, practical or legal help tailored to the circumstances. The child's experience and each adult's safety need their own attention. An agreement is useful when the people affected can actually use it.\n\n### Let the plan change with life\n\nNeeds change with health, development, work, relationships and the resources available. A plan that worked last year may need revision. Set a review that is manageable and bring one question to it. What has become harder? What support has stopped being available? What do we now understand better?\n\nThe person who depends on a plan should have a voice in changing it. Include children through communication they can use. Include the adults whose effort makes it possible. A written record can reduce the work of remembering while leaving room to adapt when circumstances change.\n\nThere will also be time for things that matter beyond managing difficulty. An interest, a walk, quiet company or a shared joke can have value in its own right. Give the plan some room for what you enjoy and what makes the day feel like yours.\n\n### A sustainable-life plan\n\nKeep this to a page, a note or another form you can return to easily.\n\n- The needs I want recognised are ____.\n- The conditions that help me participate are ____.\n- The support already available is ____.\n- The support I still need is ____.\n- The responsibilities I can realistically own are ____.\n- The recovery time those responsibilities require is ____.\n- The boundaries I need to use are ____.\n- The people or services I can ask for help are ____.\n- One thing I want more room to enjoy is ____.\n- The first change I will try is ____.\n- I will know it is helping when ____.\n- I will review it on ____.\n\nYou may finish this part with a clearer explanation, a practical request or a question that still needs attention. Each can provide a starting point. The aim is a life in which understanding reaches the arrangements you live with, the care you receive and the choices you are able to make."];
const introductionText = "This companion is written for people who have been diagnosed as neurodivergent and people who identify as neurodivergent, including those who are still exploring what that means for them. It pays particular attention to families, including partners, parents, children and the people who support them.\n\nIt is an attempt to make sense of the complexity of the interface between neurodivergence and trauma as it is experienced physically, mentally, emotionally and socially. We will explore how these experiences meet within a person, within relationships and within the demands of daily life. We will also consider what it means when disruption reaches across many parts of life at once.\n\nYou may already have language for some of your experiences. You may have received diagnoses, spent time in therapy or learnt strategies that help. You may also have questions that remain unanswered. Something can be helpful and still leave part of the picture unexplored.\n\nPerhaps you understand why a relationship disagreement feels frightening but have less understanding of why conversation becomes physically difficult. Perhaps you have learnt to recognise sensory overload while still struggling with the shame that follows asking for a break. Perhaps life has become harder in several areas, and explaining each difficulty separately fails to convey what living with all of them is like.\n\nThis companion makes room to consider those experiences together. What changes when we understand what someone is carrying before deciding what their behaviour means? The chapters explore that question through neurodivergence, trauma, relationships and parenting, with space for accommodation, autonomy and accountability.\n\n## Why families are central\n\nI come to this subject through my lived experience of neurodivergence, trauma, marriage and raising children with additional support needs. Family life is where I have encountered these complexities most directly. It is the experience I can speak from.\n\nThat perspective shapes the questions in this companion. What happens when several people in one household need support at the same time? How do you make room for recovery while children still need care? How do you understand a partner's experience while trying to communicate your own? What happens when the work of keeping everyone going becomes difficult to sustain?\n\nI write from my own position within those questions. Other members of a family have their own experiences, and their accounts matter too. Readers will bring different family structures, needs, resources and histories. I hope the language here helps them describe what fits their lives and identify what needs a different explanation.\n\nResearch and clinical knowledge sit alongside that lived perspective throughout the companion. They help me examine my interpretations, explore questions beyond my experience and be clear about where uncertainty remains.\r\n\r\n## When the effects reach across life\n\nYour body, thoughts, emotions and relationships all participate in the life you are trying to live. A difficult experience can affect several of them at once. You might feel physically unsettled, lose your place in a task, struggle to find words and become increasingly worried about what somebody thinks of you. The effort of managing one part can leave less room for another.\n\nDisruption can become global in this everyday sense. Sleep, eating, personal care, work, parenting, friendships, intimacy and the ability to organise an ordinary day can all become part of the same difficulty. What appears to be a problem with one task may sit within a much wider loss of available capacity.\n\nThis can be hard to describe to someone who sees you in one setting. They may see the appointment you attended, the work you completed or the calm explanation you gave. You may know that doing it used what you had for the rest of the day. You may then face another conversation about why the next task remains undone.\n\nUnderstanding this wider picture means asking about effort, recovery and what happens between the visible moments. It means considering the conditions in which you can participate, alongside the demands you are expected to meet.\n\n## Where neurodivergence and trauma meet\n\nThe questions in this book include both what it is like to be neurodivergent and what has happened to you as a neurodivergent person. They include how your needs have been understood, how people have responded when you expressed them and what you have learnt to expect when you need support.\n\nWe will explore experiences of repeated misunderstanding, exclusion, overwhelming environments, pressure to conceal difficulty and relationships in which asking for help has felt unsafe. We will consider how earlier experiences can become part of a present interaction, while also paying attention to what is happening in that interaction now.\n\nWe will also make room for co-occurring mental health conditions and the possibility that several explanations may each describe an important part of someone's life. The aim is to develop an understanding detailed enough to ask better questions about care, accommodation, responsibility and change.\n\nYour own account belongs in that process. You may be able to describe what is happening long before you know how to name it. “I can do this, but I cannot sustain doing it this way” is a place to begin.\n\n## The life around the person\n\nThe picture becomes more complicated when you share a life with another person whose needs and history also require care. It can become more complicated again when you are raising children with additional support needs, managing employment, navigating services or trying to find enough time and money for support.\n\nEach person has an experience from the inside. Each person's actions also affect the others. Understanding the interaction requires attention to both, including who carries the planning, whose need for rest is recognised and what happens when the available people have reached their limits.\n\nThis is where the companion meets the concerns of *When Protectors Collide*. We will examine the responses people develop to protect themselves, what those responses are trying to achieve and what happens when they meet another person's needs. Compassion, autonomy and accountability each have a place in that examination.\n\n## Reading at your own pace\n\nThe chapters offer direct explanations, short sections, practical language and optional questions. You can begin with the subject that feels most relevant and return to other material when you have room for it. You can use a question to reflect privately, prepare for an appointment or explain something that has been difficult to communicate.\n\nThe hope is that you will find language for more of your experience, recognise needs that deserve attention and see possibilities for a life that gives those needs room. That includes support for what is difficult, respect for what matters to you and a realistic understanding of what you can carry.";
